\title{Computation-Class Fidelity, Not Structure: What Sets the Closed-Loop\\ Floor of Residual World Models}

\author{Zetao Zhang\\ Quanzhou University of Information Engineering\\ \texttt{akenman1124@outlook.com}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Learned world models are trained on one-step prediction and then judged by closed-loop rollout, yet what decides whether they survive that transition is rarely measured. In two simulated worlds (a two-link underactuated acrobot pendulum and a planar ball arena), under a preregistered protocol with paired seeds, guards that bind every headline number to a script, and a measured reproduction floor, we isolate three candidates: the fidelity of the prior's integrator, the training recipe, and symbolic structure. First, the prior's computation class sets the floor. Swapping it for the class that generated the data, inside a fixed residual architecture, lowers closed-loop error from a $14.26$\,px baseline to $0.49$\,px, a $29\times$ separation whose coarse end carries $15\%$ reproduction variance; the exact prior reproduces the ball engine to $3\times10^{-5}$\,px per step, and a 401k-parameter learned model matches that floor in the pendulum world to within the measured reproduction difference. Second, the recipe multiplies within the floor: a rollout loss repairs an unstable arm by $41\times$, and where the closed-loop-loss cell was run on the exact prior (the ball world) it moves that arm by $0.2\%$, inside the floor. Over-weighting the single-step loss reverses closed-loop performance. Symbolic structure subtracts in every form we tried, and the harm vanishes once the floor is exact. Third, in the contact-rich world the trained arm sits on an interference floor: its residual head emits $0.017$--$0.027$ on the free-flight windows where the prior is already exact and its target is zero to seven decimals, a $1.2\times$ band while the priors span five orders of magnitude. The same architecture trained on the contact-free world instead scales its output with the target ($0.13$--$0.98\times$ across tiers), so the floor is a property of regime imbalance, not of the architecture. Five interventions trace one false-silence-versus-damage trade-off, and a decode-time gate restores parity with the analytic prior---on the thirty free-flight validation windows it scores---at a $4.2$--$4.7\%$ window-level false-silence rate and a $12$--$21\%$ rise in contact-regime closed-loop error. On frozen features the floor responds to loss weighting over $2.4\times$ without
closing, and the closed-loop rollout term---fit on all windows---pulls it back up
$1.6$--$4.7\times$ at matched fit, which is why the training-side levers all relocate
the error instead of removing it. The full preregistered ledger is reported, including the criteria our own claims failed. We release preregistrations, guard tests, and per-number provenance.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A world model that is meant to support control has to survive closed-loop rollout: the model consumes its own predictions, step after step, and errors compound. The standard workflow trains on one-step prediction, reports a one-step metric, and hopes. The question this paper asks is narrower than ``are world models useful''. It is: given a fixed architecture family and a fixed data budget, what decides whether a learned model survives the step from one-step fitting to closed-loop rollout?

Three answers circulate in practice. The first is architectural: give the model structure---gating, phase variables, symbolic priors---and it will roll out better. The second is the training recipe: add a rollout loss, tune the schedule. The third is rarely treated as a measured variable at all: the fidelity of an analytic prior's \emph{computation class}, by which we mean the pair of equations and the numerical scheme (integrator, step size, sampling) used to evaluate them. A prior can be exact as mathematics and still be wrong as code, if its discrete map differs from the map that produced the data. A reader may object that, in simulation, matching the generating integrator is trivially the right thing to do. It is the right thing, and it is not trivial to know what to match. Our own preregistered analytic basis for the ball engine was wrong---we had assumed the semi-implicit form the scheme's name suggests, while the engine integrates positions before velocities---and that discrepancy is larger than every ladder gap we resolve except the coarsest (\S\ref{sec:platform}).

Measuring this properly takes an instrument, so we built one. Two simulated worlds---an underactuated double pendulum and a bouncing-ball arena---with a shared architecture family in which the residual head, the trunk, and the data budget are held fixed while the prior is swapped through a registry of four integrator tiers. Twelve preregistrations, written before the runs, fix the criteria. Every number in this paper is reproduced by a script from a stored JSON and checked by guard tests, and every claim above the reproduction floor is scoped by a measured retraining variance whose worst case ($15\%$ on the five-step pendulum metric) is reported in \S\ref{sec:limitations}.

The findings are ordered by how much they surprised us. The prior ladder is monotonic and steep: in the pendulum world, exchanging the coarse integrator of the prior for the one that generated the data moves the trained arm's one-step error from 0.969\,px to 0.037\,px and closed-loop error from 14.26\,px to 0.49\,px---factors of 26 and 29 with the architecture held fixed. The same experiment in the ball world reproduces analytic predictions to four decimal places, and the exact prior there is not merely close to the engine: over five closed-loop steps it \emph{is} the engine, to $3{\times}10^{-5}$\,px per step. A learned model with 401k parameters matches this floor and cannot improve on it. The recipe, meanwhile, multiplies within the floor: a rollout loss repairs a collapsing arm by $41\times$ but changes the exact-prior arm by nothing, and there is a dose above which more single-step loss reverses closed-loop performance outright. Symbolic structure subtracts in every form we tried---gated experts across four experiment generations, and, in a two-by-two that separates supervision from capacity, the human-labelled supervision alone costs 0.269\,px (6/6 seeds)---yet when the floor is exact, that harm is absorbed almost perfectly (ratio $0.0005$).

The trained arm then adds a complication that the analytic ladder cannot show. On the windows where the exact prior is already perfect, the residual head is supposed to output zero; the supervision there is zero to four decimals, and the head's output is not. It emits roughly 0.85\,px of noise, at a level that is prior-independent to within the spread of our measurements, and this noise erases the analytic ladder in the trained arms and compounds under feedback. We tried five interventions across four preregistrations---loss reweighting, an end-to-end gate, a supervised gate, a loss-mass dose curve, and decode-time hard gating. Each moved the error somewhere else. All of them, together with the decode rule, trace one curve: a trade between silencing the head on free-flight windows and damaging contact windows. The decode rule is the useful end of that curve---it restores closed-loop parity with the analytic prior on the thirty free-flight validation windows it scores, at a window-level false-silence rate of 4.2--4.7\% and a 12--21\% rise in contact-regime closed-loop error.

This paper is a study of two simulated worlds, not a benchmark. The scale is deliberately small and the claims are scoped accordingly. What the small scale buys is control: every factor that could move the numbers is either fixed, preregistered, or guarded by a test. Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{C1 --- The floor.} In the two simulated worlds studied here, prior computational fidelity---not architecture---sets the closed-loop floor. A zero-parameter prior with the engine's integrator is exact to float32 precision in closed loop ($3\times10^{-5}$\,px per step) and ties a 401k-parameter learned model (\S\ref{sec:floor}).
  \item \textbf{C2 --- The multiplier.} Within the floor, the recipe's gain is proportional to the distance to the floor and saturates there; the dose has a ceiling and reverses past it (\S\ref{sec:substitute}, \S\ref{sec:silence}).
  \item \textbf{C3 --- The subtraction.} Within this architecture family, symbolic structure---in gating and in supervision---consistently costs accuracy; the cost vanishes at an exact floor (\S\ref{sec:substitute}).
  \item \textbf{C4 --- The interference floor and its exchange rate.} In the contact-rich world, trained-arm error decomposes into prior error plus a regime-mixing-dependent head noise that spans only $1.2\times$ while the priors span five orders of magnitude; five interventions and a decode rule lie on one ROC curve, which we quote, and on frozen features the floor responds to loss weighting without closing (\S\ref{sec:silence}, \S\ref{sec:flooranalysis}).
  \item \textbf{C5 --- Methodology as a working instrument.} Preregistered criteria, single-source numbers bound by guard tests, and a measured reproduction floor caught three of our own wrong conclusions before publication, and a single retrained arm prices the floor per metric (\S\ref{sec:platform}, \S\ref{sec:limitations}, \S\ref{sec:repro}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Reuse-and-correct methods.} A family of systems couples an analytic simulator with a learned corrector: alternating neural integrators for legacy simulators \citep{ani2026}, differentiable-simulator augmentation \citep{neuralsim2021}, residual physics for throwing \citep{tossingbot2019}, material-aware residual world models for deformable objects \citep{physcore2026}, and end-to-end identification of rigid-body dynamics from vision \citep{pinwm2025}. These works answer \emph{how to repair}. We ask the adjacent question that their evaluations presuppose: \emph{what determines whether the repair is possible, and what the repair costs}. Our interference result is a caveat their architectures inherit---the corrector's noise on the prior's home regime---and our decode-time rule is one measurable answer.

\paragraph{Structure-preserving learning.} Hamiltonian and Lagrangian networks build the integrator into the learner \citep{hgn2019,chnn2020,se3hode2021}. The concern for long-horizon stability is the same as ours; the causal question is different. We do not ask whether a learned model can be made to respect structure, but how much of closed-loop error is explained by the prior's computation class once structure is present as a candidate explanation and rejected.

\paragraph{Rollout error accumulation.} That autoregressive error compounds is a known and much-analysed property of learned dynamics \citep{pderefiner,mbpo2019,swazinna2021}. We contribute the controlled version: with the architecture and data fixed, the compounding term is governed by the prior's fidelity, it saturates at the floor, and the training-side levers that reduce one component of it reliably inflate another (\S\ref{sec:silence}).

\paragraph{Auxiliary-task interference.} Capacity loss from auxiliary losses \citep{lyle2021}, gradient-magnitude balancing across tasks \citep{metabalance2022}, and expert-utilisation pathology in mixture-of-experts \citep{deepseekv3,auxlossfree2024} are the nearest neighbours of our interference floor. The instance here is new---a residual head that will not stay silent where an exact prior leaves it nothing to do---and we test the obvious fixes causally rather than proposing another balancer.

\paragraph{Handing authority back to a deterministic floor.} Handing authority to a fallback controller is established in control: predictive safety filters verify a backup trajectory online and revert to it when a learned policy risks constraint violation \citep{wabersich2021}, and control-barrier-function filters modulate a nominal controller to keep a safe set invariant \citep{ames2019}; abstention and conformal wrappers extend the idea under uncertainty. Our decode-time gate is that idea applied to a dynamics world model, with two additions: the regime indicator is physically interpretable (a contact indicator, not an epistemic score), and the cost of handback on in-distribution performance is measured as an explicit exchange rate rather than left implicit.

\paragraph{Discretisation bias is classical; its size beside architecture is not.} That a model estimated from sampled data of a continuous-time process absorbs the discretisation into its parameters is classical in system identification \citep{ljung1999}. Our identification experiment reproduces exactly that behaviour ($\hat g = 705.7$ against a generating $900$ on the clean world, and $871$--$886$ against $1125$ on the perturbed ones), and we read the classical result as the reason to expect it rather than as a finding. What the classical treatment does not supply is the comparison this paper is built around: with architecture, data, recipe and seeds fixed, how large the absorbed bias is next to the levers a practitioner actually chooses between---the prior's computation class, symbolic structure, and loss geometry---and how it translates into closed-loop error.

\paragraph{Implicit identification is also classical; its price on the easy regime is not.} A residual head that learns a state-dependent correction to a parametric prior is doing online parameter estimation implicitly, and that too has a classical lineage \citep{ljung1999}. Appendix~\ref{app:corrector} shows the correspondence directly: when the prior's parameter is wrong by $25\%$, a head---trained on a trunk that never saw the mismatch---recovers $93\%$ of the closed-loop error, which is what an implicit estimator should do. The classical literature tells us to expect the recovery; what it does not price is what the same estimator does on the windows where the parameter is already right. That price is the subject of \S\ref{sec:silence}, and it is the part of this paper we take to be new.

This paper studies \emph{dynamics-level} world models for control; video- and LLM-scale world models evaluate on different semantics \citep{wm_survey2026} and are out of scope.

\section{Experimental Platform}\label{sec:platform}

\subsection{Two worlds}

\textbf{Pendulum.} An underactuated double pendulum in the acrobot configuration: two links ($L=80$\,px, point masses 2.0 and 1.0), gravity 900\,px/s$^2$, one normalised elbow torque bounded at 28{,}000. Ground truth integrates the Lagrangian equations with RK4 at 0.01\,s and samples at 20\,Hz. The state is 25 dimensions per window step: two 11-dim object segments (position, angle as sin/cos, linear and angular velocity, constant attributes) plus a three-channel contact block. Three thousand episodes of 10\,s. The prior is torque-driven and contact-free by construction, which makes this world the clean instrument: its exact tier can be checked against the engine frame by frame.

\textbf{Ball.} A planar arena simulated with a rigid-body engine: three dynamic bodies, static obstacles in eight visible slots, gravity 900\,px/s$^2$, actions recorded as per-body net impulses over each 0.05\,s interval, applied as constant forces at every 0.01\,s engine step. Fifty thousand episodes of 200 frames. The state assembles to 116 dimensions (33 object, 80 obstacle, 3 contact). This world adds what the pendulum lacks: contact. Its free-flight segments are the only place where a contact-free prior can be exact, and its engine turned out to be instructive in its own right, for a reason we did not expect. The arena runs on pymunk/Chipmunk2D, whose documentation states that it ``uses standard Euler to perform the integration'' and spells out the order the integrator works in: positions are integrated \emph{before} velocities \citep{pymunk2025}. A transfer therefore accumulates $10a\Delta t^2$ rather than the $15a\Delta t^2$ that integrating velocities first (semi-implicit, and the form the scheme's name suggests) would give. Our preregistration had assumed the semi-implicit form; the data forced the correction, and at 900\,px/s$^2$ the two signatures differ by 0.45\,px per transfer---almost three times the finest gap the ball ladder resolves in \S\ref{sec:floor} (0.159\,px). The lesson is not that this engine's documentation and code disagree. It is that a plausible prior about an engine's discrete map can be wrong by more than the effect being measured, and that only the data settle it. All quantities are in the engine's native pixel units; converting them would add a constant to every tier and cannot change a comparison, so we do not.

\subsection{Architecture family}

All arms share a GRU trunk (hidden 256 unless stated) with LayerNorm, consuming the assembled window ($L{=}8$ past frames). The residual parameterisation is fixed throughout: the head predicts a correction to the prior's output, $\mathrm{pred} = \mathrm{prior}(s_t, a_t) + \mathrm{head}(h_t, a_t)$, with the head's final layer small-initialised so that training starts at the prior. Around this trunk we register fifteen arms for the pendulum (pure learning, strong MLP, JEPA-style, gated, ablations, four analytic tiers) and the trained ladder plus two silence mechanisms for the ball. Parameter budgets are levelled to within $\pm 10\%$ of the base arm; the wiring of every arm is asserted from its state dictionary, not from its configuration, by guard tests (\S\ref{sec:repro}). Across the arms reported here, model sizes run from 339k to 615k parameters: the pendulum models sit at 387k--431k, the ball models at 598k with the two gate arms of \S\ref{sec:silence} at 615k, and the capacity ablations of \S\ref{sec:substitute} span 339k--470k by design rather than by budget.

\subsection{The prior registry}

Each world has a registry with four prior tiers---for the pendulum, semi-implicit Euler at 0.05\,s, Euler at 0.01$\times$5, RK4 at 0.05\,s, RK4 at 0.01$\times$5; for the ball, the legacy closed-form parabola, explicit Euler at $0.05{\times}1$, RK4 at $0.05{\times}1$, explicit Euler at $0.01{\times}5$. The registry is the single implementation: a numpy path builds training targets, a torch path serves evaluation, and a test asserts the two agree to within $10^{-4}$ and that the default tier reproduces the historical implementation bit for bit. The tier used to build the residual target during training is the same tier the model evaluates with; a mismatch there would silently invalidate every comparison, so it is guarded, not promised.

\subsection{Protocol}

Every experiment in this paper that belongs to one of the twelve preregistered slots ran under a preregistration written before the first run: design, seeds, thresholds with their calibration source, stopping rules, and what counts as a crashed run. Comparisons are paired by seed; crashed runs stay in the analysis (ITT) and their rate is reported. In practice no run crashed: every slot that stores the field records an ITT rate of $0.0$ (S0b, S1b, S1d, S1e, S1f), and the supervision $2\times2$ records $0/24$. There is one exception to that accounting, and it applies to the gating series of \S\ref{sec:substitute}, which predates the twelve preregistrations: those arms additionally report stable-run means with collapsed runs excluded, because a collapsed run's saved checkpoint is systematically worse than a completed one's. The report that defines them (\texttt{E1a\_corrected\_report.json}) also carries the full-sample reading and marks the criterion \textsc{disagree} rather than choosing between the two; the crash rates that rule produces are unequal across arms ($0/10$, $1/10$, $3/10$ for three of the variants), which is why both readings are reported rather than one. The run-to-run reproduction floor was measured by retraining configurations from identical settings and comparing to the first runs. On one-step error it is small---$0.5\%$ on the retrained arm, and the analytic prior reproduces bit for bit---and on the pendulum's closed-loop $k{=}2$ it is $0.2\%$. On the five-step closed-loop metric of \S\ref{sec:floor} it is not: the same arm, seed and hardware retrained to a different selected epoch moves from $14.07$ to $16.18$\,px, $15\%$. We therefore quote closed-loop differences only at the scale of the $29\times$ ladder of Table~\ref{tab:ladder}, and we do not treat any closed-loop gap below that as a measurement; \S\ref{sec:limitations} carries the per-quantity numbers. Our three-to-six seed budget is at the low end of what the power-analysis literature recommends \citep{colas2018}, which is why the guard is directional replication against preregistered absolute thresholds rather than significance claims. Every headline number in this paper descends from a stored JSON report through a single build script; 40 guard tests (prior-registry cross-checks, document-to-JSON consistency, checkpoint-naming and overwrite protection, wiring assertions read back from state dictionaries) make divergence between the paper's numbers and the artifacts a test failure rather than a possibility. Twelve preregistrations accompany the paper, the mechanism probe of \S\ref{sec:silence} included. Three of our own earlier conclusions were overturned inside this protocol before publication---a gating advantage, a loss-quality claim, and a dose--response expectation---and all three are described in the body (\S\ref{sec:substitute}, \S\ref{sec:silence}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}) because they are part of the result.

\section{The Prior Ladder Sets the Floor}\label{sec:floor}

\subsection{A ladder in the pendulum world}

We first trained the residual architecture on the pendulum world with four priors, everything else fixed (60 epochs, three seeds). One-step error follows the prior tier monotonically: 0.969, 0.196, 0.136, 0.037\,px as the integrator moves from coarse Euler to the engine's own RK4 scheme; closed-loop error at five steps follows it from 14.26 to 0.49\,px (Table~\ref{tab:ladder}). One caveat belongs on that second column: the coarse-prior arm's five-step value carries $15\%$ run-to-run variance under retraining (\S\ref{sec:platform}), so the ladder is quoted as a $29\times$ separation and not as four precise numbers; the exact tier's own cross-seed standard deviation is $0.009$\,px, three orders of magnitude below the differences the ladder rests on. The exact tier beats the preregistered thresholds on all four criteria (Table~\ref{tab:criteria}). Two details deserve emphasis. The residual head's contribution to closed-loop error is not stable---across tiers it ranges from a 10\% improvement over its own prior to a 50\% degradation---while the prior tiers themselves differ by $32\times$ (the trained arms, $29\times$). And the exact tier removes the seed variance almost entirely: closed-loop standard deviation across seeds falls from 1.3--2.4\,px across the middle tiers to 0.01\,px at the exact one. When the prior is right, the trained arm stops being a random variable.

