\subsection{The zero-parameter ladder crosses engines}

The trained ladder conflates two things: the quality of the prior and whatever the head does with it. So we removed the head. Four zero-parameter priors were evaluated on the ball world's contact-free transfers (6{,}597 transfers passing a contact-flag and velocity-consistency filter), with the pendulum's three-tier analytic validation as precedent.

The engine check comes first and it is the reason to trust everything after it. The ball engine's exact tier---explicit Euler at 0.01\,s, five substeps, constant per-step forces---reproduces the recorded transitions to $3{\times}10^{-5}$\,px one-step, and rollouts over five closed-loop steps reach $1.4{\times}10^{-4}$\,px. A zero-parameter prior reproduces the learned data-generating process to float32 precision ($3\times10^{-5}$\,px per step) at deployment sampling (Fig.~\ref{fig:ladder}b).

The ladder itself, on free-flight windows: the legacy closed-form parabola and the RK4 tier tie at 0.1591\,px; the same-family coarse tier sits at 0.6364\,px. Both values were predicted analytically before the run: the parabola and RK4 are the exact solution of the constant-acceleration problem and miss the engine's per-transfer position gain by $2.5a\Delta t^2$ along the gravity axis (0.225\,px at $a{=}900$), the coarse tier by $10a\Delta t^2$ (0.9\,px); pooled over the six position dimensions, of which gravity moves three, the predicted pos-RMSE is these over $\sqrt{2}$---0.1591 and 0.6364\,px---and the measurements reproduce them to the fourth decimal. Two readings matter. First, the mathematically exact solution is not the computationally matched one: the parabola solves the ODE perfectly and is still 0.159\,px wrong at every step, because the engine's discrete map is not the flow. Second, matching the family without matching the step size does not help; the same-family tier is the worst of the four. On mixed windows that include contact, all four tiers compress to 0.9--1.2\,px, which is the contact mismatch asserting itself---a second, additive error source that no integrator choice addresses.

