\subsection{The aliasing law, across seeds}

The identification experiments (\S\ref{sec:silence} uses their architecture) put a number on what \S\ref{sec:floor} implies. A Nelder--Mead identifier asked to fit the prior's parameters under a multi-step rollout objective, given clean data whose true gravity is $900$, returns $\hat g = 705.7$---not $900$. The value is a property of the prior's computation class rather than of the sample. Two measurements separate the two. The clean dataset is fixed by the preregistration, so all three runs return $705.7333$, identical to the last digit---that is a design fact, not evidence of sample-independence. The evidence is the perturbed worlds: on three independently regenerated worlds with gravity at $1125$, the same class returns $870.9$, $880.7$ and $886.4$ (a $1.8\%$ spread, $21$--$23\%$ below the generating value), and refining the class moves it to $957.3$, $961.4$ and $958.5$ ($0.4\%$ spread, still $15\%$ low). Lengthening the identification horizon walks it monotonically in every seed: $593$--$622$ at $H{=}1$, $871$--$886$ at $H{=}5$, $923$--$952$ at $H{=}10$. We do not claim the bias itself as new---that discrete-time estimates absorb the discretisation is classical \citep{ljung1999}---but its reproducibility across independently generated worlds, and its size relative to the architecture and recipe levers of \S\ref{sec:floor} and \S\ref{sec:substitute}, are what the ladder makes measurable. The practical form of this law is a specification: an identification or filtering pipeline that does not model the prior's computation class is fitting the mismatch, and it will do so reproducibly.

\subsection{What the floor buys}

With the exact prior in place, the residual head has nothing measurable to learn in the pendulum world: the trained arm reaches 0.037\,px against its prior's 0.038, and the closed-loop values are 0.49 against 0.49. A 401k-parameter learned model ends where a zero-parameter one begins. We treat this as the clean statement of C1: the floor is real, it is set by computation rather than physics, and in a contact-free world it is the whole story. The rest of the paper is about what happens when it is not.

\section{Structure and Recipe Cannot Substitute}\label{sec:substitute}

\subsection{The recipe multiplies within the floor}

The pendulum arms in \S\ref{sec:floor} were all trained single-step. If closed-loop failure is a training problem, a rollout loss should fix it. The preregistered answer took four arms across three recipe cells---single-step, closed-loop loss at the original learning rate, and closed-loop loss with the rate halved---and the gains track how unstable the arm was rather than how far it sat from the floor. The collapsing arm (pure GRU, closed-loop 53.89\,px) improves $41\times$ under the rollout loss; the coarse-prior arm improves $2.9\times$; the arm that was already stable---a strong MLP at 1.65\,px, $3.4\times$ above the exact arm's floor---gains nothing measurable (0\%, $t=-0.37$, $p=0.75$). That is the honest limit of the recipe: it repairs collapsing arms, and it does not reach the floor. The exact-prior arm was not run in the third cell, by preregistration, because there was nothing for the loss to act on. The best non-exact arm in any cell, 1.31\,px, remains $2.7\times$ above the exact arm's 0.49.

The cell design earned its keep by accident. The original two-cell plan (with the learning rate halved alongside the rollout loss) would have credited the loss for a degradation that belonged to the learning rate: single-step error rose in the halved-rate cell for every arm. The third cell separated the two, and the corrected reading---the rollout loss does not damage one-step accuracy at all---is one of the three self-corrections this protocol produced.

\subsection{Symbolic structure subtracts}

The gated arm---64 phase-gated experts modulating the residual head through FiLM---has now been tested across four experiment generations: the unified main table, a ten-seed gating ablation, a capacity-decoupled variant, and a paired meta-analysis over seven gating variants. The gating term is a cost in all of them: $+0.161$\,px paired (95\% interval $[+0.116, +0.205]$ across variants), direction consistent in 7/7 variants---variants that share seeds and architecture, so we read the count as one replicated finding rather than seven independent ones, and the interval is the spread across those variants rather than a sampling confidence interval---with a routing audit showing that the gate leaves most of its experts unused (2 of 64 occupied in the decoupled variant, 9--15 of 64 in the fully coupled one). One caution about the number usually quoted here: the phase labels are themselves imbalanced---89\% of windows fall in three of eight classes and two classes never occur---and that histogram is identical for every arm, the ungated ones included, so it describes the data rather than the gate (Table~\ref{tab:c3}). A free (unstructured) gate costs the same, which rules out mis-chosen semantics as the culprit. The full negative-result catalogue is in Appendix~\ref{app:negative}.

The $2\times2$ in Table~\ref{tab:p0c} separates the other channel, supervision, from capacity. Holding the architecture fixed and switching only loss weights, the label-supervised variants (C10, C11) are worse than the unsupervised ones (C00, C01) by 0.269\,px paired (95\% CI $[+0.137, +0.402]$), 6/6 seeds, $p=0.011$; the self-supervised prototype family contributes nothing measurable ($+0.022$\,px, CI $[-0.011, +0.055]$, 4/6 seeds). The supervision check confirms the mechanism was live---the supervised cells' indicator accuracy is 0.968 against 0.474 for the unsupervised cells---so the cost is paid by a head that demonstrably learned what we asked it to learn (Fig.~\ref{fig:p0c}). The reading we stand behind: phase labels are a description the state head does not need, and asking the trunk to predict them taxes the prediction we actually want.

