\subsection{Reweighting buys one-step silence; an end-to-end gate starves}

The first intervention reweights the state loss on contact-flag-free windows (both the last input frame and the target frame contact-free) by a fixed $\times10$, chosen before the run. It works on the term it acts on: free-flight one-step error falls from 0.856 to 0.461\,px, the head's free-flight output RMS halves ($0.0186 \to 0.0095$; the single-seed probe reading in Table~\ref{tab:probe} is $0.0197$ for the same quantity on seed 42, a $6\%$ difference on a quantity whose reproduction difference is $0.5\%$), and---the part we did not expect---mixed error does not degrade ($0.570 \to 0.547$, a 4\% shift inside the reproduction floor), so the contact regime paid nothing. Closed-loop free-flight error falls from 2.042 to 1.406 but misses its preregistered threshold (1.02). The second intervention adds a dedicated pathway: a per-sample gate between head and prior, trained end-to-end. It fails for a measurable reason. The regime mask is live on about 4.5\% of training windows (the stricter velocity-consistent subset that the evaluation scores is 0.4\%); the gradient that would teach the gate to close is outnumbered twenty to one, and the gate stays at its initialisation. Silence is purchasable with loss mass; a pathway alone is not.

\subsection{A supervised gate learns the regime but not the silence}

If starvation is the problem, supervise the gate directly: the regime label exists in the data as the contact flag, so we trained the gate with a binary cross-entropy against it, one preregistered arm. The gate learned the regime almost perfectly---99.34\% of windows, 95.5\% of free-flight windows closed. The silence did not follow: free-flight one-step error landed at 0.545\,px, closed-loop at 1.596, both worse than plain reweighting. The probe explains it. The gate is a soft attenuator---its mean on free-flight windows is 0.13 to 0.29, never zero---and the state loss constrains only the product of gate and residual. The head scaled its output up by $1.6$--$1.9\times$ to compensate, and the product, which is all that training sees, moved less than reweighting had moved it. Gate and head co-adapt. Two interventions, two lessons, one conclusion forming: the loss geometry, not the architecture, is the causal variable.

\subsection{Loss mass has a dose--response ceiling, and it reverses}

The third preregistration mapped the dose. Three arms held the exact prior and varied which loss term carried the extra mass: closed-loop loss $\times10$ (one-step untouched by design), both terms $\times10$, and single-step $\times100$. The two-component structure of the error shows up cleanly. Closed-loop mass attacks exactly the compounding term---closed-loop falls 24.9\% while the one-step free-flight error moves 1.8\% ($0.856 \to 0.872$, inside the one-step reproduction difference) and mixed one-step rises $0.570 \to 0.602$, inside its preregistered 0.628\,px bound. Both terms together give the best closed-loop of the six arms compared here (Table~\ref{tab:arms}; 1.329\,px) at a small one-step cost against its $\times10$ single-step reference (0.613 against 0.461\,px). And the single-step arm delivers the best free-flight one-step error of the study, 0.306\,px, while its closed-loop error \emph{reverses} to 2.727\,px---34\% worse than the unmodified baseline and 94\% worse than the $\times10$ arm it was derived from. The per-step profile is the mechanism: its error grows $2.6$--$3.9\times$ per feedback step and then $4.5$--$5.1\times$ on the next, in every seed, accelerating with depth, where no other arm's second-step growth exceeds $2.6\times$. Given enough single-step mass, the optimizer produced a head that is accurate once and unstable under its own feedback. We had the dose--response curve backwards in our preregistered expectation, and the reversal, not the improvement, is the finding: the two error components do not trade against each other monotonically, because the head is shared and the loss geometry overfits (Fig.~\ref{fig:dose}).

\subsection{Decode-time hard gating redeems the closed loop, at a quoted rate}

The four training-side interventions share a failure mode: they act through the shared head, so whatever they buy on free-flight windows is paid for on contact windows, and the exchange is not under control. The fifth intervention steps out of training entirely. At decode time, per rollout step, if the maximum contact-indicator probability over objects exceeds a threshold $\tau$, the head is used; below it, the head is skipped and the analytic prior acts alone. The threshold is selected on the first half of the validation windows (val-A), under a rule that bounds false silencing at 2\%, and all verdicts are computed on the second half (val-B).

The result closes the arc. Free-flight closed-loop error under the rule is $10^{-4}$\,px, in every seed, for the trained arms and for the unmodified baseline alike---parity with the analytic prior, from a trained model, with no retraining (Fig.~\ref{fig:roc}). Two qualifications keep the claim honest. The scored windows are few: the second validation half contains 15, 4 and 11 free-flight closed-loop windows across the three seeds, some thirty in all. The claim is therefore that the rule silenced every window it scored, and the residual transfer cost is small: across arms and seeds the val-B false-silence rate at the selected threshold runs 1.80--2.13\% (baseline arm: 1.92--2.01\%), against the 1.96\% mean quoted below; the heavier evidence sits in the threshold scan that selects $\tau$ (22--26 free-flight windows per seed per threshold, and some 72{,}000 free-flight window-steps). Nor does the rule redeem every arm: the dose-reversed $\times100$ arm's indicator inherits its degraded head, 4--22\% of its free-flight windows stay open at $\tau{=}0.9$, and the few open windows carry RMS 0.23--1.55\,px---silence presupposes a head whose errors are confined to the contact regime, and that arm no longer meets the premise. The indicator needs no training because it was always trained: the contact head's free-flight output probability is 0.040 on average against 0.973 on contact windows, and the $\tau$ rule merely reads it out. The cost is quoted with the same precision. The 2\% of contact windows falsely silenced lose the head's correction, and contact-regime closed-loop error rises by a factor of 1.12--1.21 across arms, missing the $\times1.10$ tolerance we preregistered. Appendix~\ref{app:silence_cost} closes that accounting per window, and shows why a two-percent rate is enough: the windows the rule silences are the high-error ones. Training the indicator for a better curve (two arms, preregistered) moved the protection rate by 0.8 percentage points---inside the reproduction floor of the metric it is read on---and left the curve where it was. One preregistered detail deviates in the shipped artifacts: the $\tau$ grid was registered at 0.50--0.99 in steps of 0.01 and shipped at 0.50--0.95 in steps of 0.05.

