\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{figs/fig5_silence_roc.pdf}
\caption{The exchange rate of silence. Each point is one arm at one decode threshold $\tau$ (validation half); the $x$-axis is the false-silence rate on contact windows (\%) and the $y$-axis is contact-regime closed-loop damage relative to the same arm ungated. The three arms' curves coincide. Stars mark the preregistered selection rule's working points; the dashed line is the $\times1.10$ tolerance.}
\label{fig:roc}
\end{figure}

\subsection{One curve, one exchange rate}

Put the seven trained arms and the decode rule on one plot (Fig.~\ref{fig:dose}, Fig.~\ref{fig:roc}) and they do not scatter: they lie on a trade-off between free-flight silence and contact damage. Reweighting and the dose curve move along it from inside training; the decode rule reaches its far end from outside, by construction, and is the only point that achieves analytic parity at all. The exchange rate is the summary, and we give it as a measurement rather than an adjective: along the decode-rule scan, the false-silence percentage points paid per decade of free-flight closed-loop error purchased. On the baseline arm, moving $\tau$ from 0.5 to the silence floor buys 3.6--4.0 decades ($0.40$--$0.81$\,px down to $10^{-4}$\,px) for 0.6--1.2 percentage points of false silence (per seed: 1.23, 0.71, 0.55---the floor arrives early where the indicator separates cleanly), a price of $0.15$--$0.31$ percentage points per decade (per seed: $0.31$, $0.18$, $0.15$); the preregistered operating point---the largest $\tau$ whose false-silence rate stays at or below 2\%---sits at 1.9--2.0\% with the floor reached. From the unmasked baseline the whole purchase is 4.3 decades ($2.04 \to 10^{-4}$\,px). Whether a better regime indicator could shift the curve itself remains open---our one attempt moved it by less than the reproduction floor of the metric it is read on---but a practitioner choosing a deployment point no longer needs to guess the price.

