\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{A specification for hybrid deployment.} The practitioner's form of C1 is a checklist item: when a world model operates next to a simulator, the simulator's computation class---integrator, step size, action sampling---must match the data-generating process at the scale being evaluated, or the floor the model inherits is the mismatch, not the physics. Our aliasing result sharpens this from advice to measurement: a fitted parameter that lands reproducibly off the true value ($\hat g = 705.7$ against $900$ on the clean world, with a $0.4\%$ spread across independently regenerated worlds) is not an estimation failure but a property of the class, and refining the class moves it in the direction the law predicts. A reader may still object that, in simulation, matching the generating integrator is tautological. The matching is easy to state and hard to know: the engine is a known discrete map only to someone who reads its code or tests it frame by frame---our own analytic basis for it was wrong until the data corrected it (\S\ref{sec:platform})---and nothing in the pipeline but the data revealed that. With real sensor data there is no code to read at all, and the identification law of \S\ref{sec:floor} is the honest analogue: fit the class, not the flow.

\paragraph{Interference is not specific to this architecture.} We found the floor with a GRU residual head and mapped it with five interventions, but the mechanism---a shared head trained on a regime-imbalanced mixture will not be silent on the minority regime---is a statement about loss geometry, and the balancing literature has met its cousins in other clothes \citep{lyle2021,metabalance2022}. The measured exchange rate is the part we believe generalises first: any proposal to make a residual head regime-quiet should be asked what it pays on the majority regime, in the currency of the metric that matters. We have staked the question, not the number: every measurement here comes from one architecture family in two planar worlds, and the exchange rate is what the next architecture should be asked to reproduce, not evidence that it will.

\paragraph{What remains of the symbolic programme.} Phase labels failed as gating signal, failed as supervision, and their occupancy pathology is structural. What survives is the audit use: the indicator that the decode rule reads is a trained phase head, and the occupancy table diagnosed the gating pathology before any accuracy number did. Cultural priors, in our experience, earn their place as instruments, not components. The filtering direction---maintaining beliefs over hidden states with an explicit computation-class prior---connects to Neural Bayesian Filtering~\citep{nbf2025}, which we defer to future work.

\paragraph{Discipline as an error-correction mechanism.} Three conclusions in this paper's history died inside the protocol: a gating advantage that was two negative arms compared to each other, a loss-quality claim that a third cell attributed to the learning rate, and a dose--response expectation that the reversal disproved. Each died cheaply, before publication, because the criteria were written first and the numbers had one source. We consider the protocol the second result of the paper.

\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Scale and domain.} Two simulated worlds---a two-link underactuated (acrobot) pendulum and a planar ball arena---with models between 401k and 598k parameters. Every claim is scoped to these. The interference mechanism and the aliasing law are statements about loss geometry and computation classes that we expect to travel, but nothing here tests them at scale, in three dimensions, or on hardware. Recurrent world models of this family remain competitive at the state of the art \citep{dreamerv3_2025}, which bounds how much of our conclusion is an artefact of model size.

\textbf{Sample size and the reproduction floor.} Trained comparisons use three to six seeds, paired. What we report is paired effects with confidence intervals (Tables~\ref{tab:p0c}, \ref{tab:c3}) and their direction across seeds. \citet{colas2018} show that a budget this small cannot support significance claims on small differences, and our own reproduction check is more specific than theirs. Retraining one arm from identical settings on the same hardware reproduces the analytic prior exactly ($0.00\%$), one-step error to $0.5\%$ and closed-loop $k{=}2$ to $0.2\%$, but its five-step closed-loop error moves from $14.07$ to $16.18$\,px---$15\%$, because the two runs select different best epochs, 35 and 59. That is the metric the ladder's second column is built on, and it is the least reproducible quantity we measured. The floor is therefore metric-dependent as well as arm-dependent: the middle-tier trained arms carry cross-seed standard deviations of $1.3$--$2.4$\,px on this metric while the exact tier carries $0.009$\,px. We handle it by scope rather than by correction---every closed-loop claim in this paper is a $29\times$ or larger separation, or a $34\%$ reversal, and no rounding to four significant figures is load-bearing.

\textbf{The silence line ends at a trade-off.} We did not achieve full closed-loop silence with training alone, and the one indicator-training attempt moved the ROC by less than the reproduction floor of the metric it is read on. The decode rule is a deployment answer with a quoted cost, not a training answer.

\textbf{Mechanism status.} The interference floor is measured, not derived, and
\S\ref{sec:flooranalysis} both removes three candidate explanations---representation
cost, loss mass, and a flat head on free-flight inputs---and identifies one term that
does counteract silence, the rollout loss. Two things stay open. The attainable
endpoint of the refit is underdetermined within roughly a factor of two: five refits
of one configuration, varying the random-number execution order and the training-pool
size, span $0.28$--$0.58$ of the unmodified head, so we quote a band and not a value.
And the representational component is inferred from repeated refits on one checkpoint
rather than measured as a property of the feature space, so we cannot say how much of
the floor is the trunk and how much is the objective. The subtraction result's cause
(why labels tax the trunk) is likewise unproven; we separated it from capacity and
measured its size.

\textbf{The interference floor is a property of regime imbalance, not of the architecture.} We tested this directly by measuring the same trained heads' free-flight output on the pendulum, where the residual targets of four priors span $100\times$. There the head's output tracks its target at ratios of $0.13$--$0.98$; in the ball world the same ratio runs from $1.0$ to $1.4\times10^{5}$ (Appendix~\ref{app:corrector}). A head that scales with its target and a head that ignores it are two regimes, and our measurements locate the regime boundary in whether the minority window's target is small compared with the majority's. This means the interference floor should be read as a statement about contact-rich, regime-imbalanced training, and we do not claim it for worlds where the loss is not dominated by a regime whose windows look different.

\textbf{The negative result about the corrector is scoped, not general.} Every claim in
this paper about the residual head being worthless comes from priors that are wrong only
through their discretisation, which is a per-window exact error and therefore leaves
nothing systematic to learn. Appendix~\ref{app:corrector} varies the kind of mismatch
instead of its magnitude and finds the head removing $89$--$97\%$ of the closed-loop
error when the prior's parameter is wrong or a force is missing
(Table~\ref{tab:corrector}), with the control
(matched prior) returning $-0.4\%$. A reader should take this paper's negative
results---and the interference floor in particular---as statements about what a learned
corrector costs on the regime where the prior is already right, not as evidence that it
is generally without value. Appendix~\ref{app:corrector} is post-hoc and single-run per
cell; we flag it here rather than in the body precisely because it scopes our own claims
and has not been through the protocol.

\textbf{Protocol scope.} Validation splits vary per seed (paired analysis only), windows-per-epoch was capped at 150k for cost, and the closed-loop evaluation window is five steps (pendulum) and three steps (ball)---short by design, since the pendulum's chaos saturates point predictions beyond it. The reproduction floor of the previous paragraph is estimated from a single retrained arm, and the free-flight windows that the decode rule scores are scarce enough that its headline number rests on some thirty windows (\S\ref{sec:silence}).

\section{Reproducibility Statement}\label{sec:repro}

The repository ships the two world generators with their validation suites, the full training and evaluation pipeline, and a manifest of 289 model checkpoints, each with a 16-character SHA-256 digest, across the 17 roots behind the claims here. It also ships twelve preregistration documents and 40 guard tests: prior-registry cross-implementation checks, document-to-JSON number consistency, checkpoint naming and overwrite protection, and architecture wiring assertions read back from state dictionaries. Every headline number in this paper is produced by a named script from a stored JSON in the analysis directory, and Appendix~\ref{app:provenance} lists the automated check that compares 148 of them against their sources, currently 148/148. The supplementary archive contains the manuscript sources, the twelve preregistrations with an English summary of every criterion they fix (\texttt{PREREG\_CRITERIA\_EN.md}), the guard suite, the citation audit, and every stored JSON report quoted here; the trajectory datasets and model checkpoints (over 300\,MB) are excluded from the archive for size and are required by the training pipeline and by two of the provenance checks. All trained arms run on a single 8\,GB consumer GPU; the full experimental matrix is roughly forty GPU-hours. Preregistrations were committed before the first run of each of the twelve slots. The gating series of \S\ref{sec:substitute} predates them and has no separate preregistration file in the release; its criteria, crash rule and both readings are carried in \texttt{E1a\_corrected\_report.json}, and we flag that as the one place where the paper reports an experiment whose criteria are documented only in its own analysis report. The two exceptions to strict pairing (per-seed validation splits, fixed identification windows) are declared in the preregistrations themselves. One preregistered detail deviates in the shipped artifacts: the SLOT-1f threshold grid was registered at 0.50--0.99 in steps of 0.01 and shipped at 0.50--0.95 in steps of 0.05. The floor analysis of \S\ref{sec:flooranalysis} is explicitly \emph{not} preregistered: it was run after the twelve slots, uses no new trained arms (it refits a head on frozen features from an existing checkpoint), and is reported as post-hoc mechanism work in a section that labels every claim it makes as measured or as an untested hypothesis.

\paragraph{Use of AI assistance.} Large language models were used for code drafting, prose editing, and literature retrieval under human direction. The experimental designs, preregistered criteria, and thresholds were specified by the authors; every quantitative claim is bound to a stored JSON by an executable guard script, and the guard suite (40 pytest guard tests plus 148 number-provenance checks) is part of the released artifacts.

