\section{When the corrector is worth having}\label{app:corrector}
\emph{This appendix reports work run after the twelve slots, in the same spirit as
\S\ref{sec:flooranalysis}: explicitly not preregistered, and included because it
scopes the paper's own negative claims.}

The body reports that the residual head is worth nothing on the free-flight windows of
the exact-prior arm---$+0.4\%$ on one step, $-0.4\%$ in closed loop---and that it is
actively harmful in the ball world. Those measurements share one property: in both, the
prior is wrong only through its discretisation, an error that is \emph{per-window exact}
under a fixed substep and a constant action, so there is no systematic residual for a
head to learn. We therefore varied the \emph{kind} of mismatch rather than its
magnitude, holding the architecture, the data budget, the schedule and the window
convention fixed, and measured the head's contribution under three priors: one that is
exactly right, one whose gravity parameter is wrong by $25\%$, and one that omits a
drag and Coulomb-friction term present in the data. The window convention, action index
and metric follow \S\ref{sec:platform}'s evaluation path and reproduce its published
prior error to three decimals ($0.0377$\,px against $0.038$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{The head's contribution as a function of what is wrong with the prior
(pendulum, seed 42, one run per cell, weighted position RMSE in px). ``Matched'' is the
control: the prior is numerically correct, and the head returns $-0.4\%$ in closed loop,
reproducing the body's claim. Under a wrong parameter or an unmodelled force the head
removes $89$--$97\%$ of the closed-loop error it is handed. The last two rows separate
which part of the model does the work: freezing the trunk on \emph{matched} data---so
its hidden state cannot encode the mismatch---recovers almost all of the gain, which
places the contribution in the head rather than the trunk.}
\label{tab:corrector}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
Condition & What is wrong with the prior & one-step (px) & closed $k{=}5$ (px) & $k{=}5$ gain \\
\midrule
prior & --- & 0.0377 & 0.4939 & --- \\
\quad + head & nothing (control) & 0.0428 & 0.4959 & $-0.4\%$ \\
\midrule
prior & gravity off by $25\%$ & 0.2123 & 5.0409 & --- \\
\quad + head & & 0.0516 & 0.5410 & $+89.3\%$ \\
\quad + head, frozen trunk & & 0.0443 & 0.3649 & $+92.8\%$ \\
\midrule
prior & unmodelled drag and friction & 0.2335 & 5.1126 & --- \\
\quad + head & & 0.0177 & 0.1569 & $+96.9\%$ \\
\quad + head, frozen trunk & & 0.0275 & 0.1905 & $+96.3\%$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two things follow for how the paper should be read. First, the negative result about the
head is specific rather than general: it holds when the prior's only error is its
discretisation, and it reverses by an order of magnitude when the prior is wrong in a way
that is smooth, persistent and inferable from observations. A reader should not conclude
that residual correction is worthless; the correct conclusion is that it is worth
precisely as much as the prior is wrong \emph{learnably}, and nothing at all on the
windows where the prior is already right. Second, the mechanism is implicit parameter
identification performed by the head: the trunk is not required to encode the mismatch,
since a head trained on a trunk fitted to matched data recovers the same gain. That
places this paper's contribution next to the classical online-identification literature
rather than apart from it---the head is doing parameter estimation implicitly, and what
this paper adds is the cost of doing it on the regime where no estimation is needed.
That ordering also has a practical consequence we did not expect. Freezing the trunk
and adapting only the head is not merely as good as training both: on the
wrong-parameter prior it is \emph{better}, lowering closed-loop error from $0.488$ to
$0.390$ averaged over four seeds, $25.4\%$ with a per-seed spread of $6.2\%$
($t=8.2$), in the same direction on $4/4$. On the unmodelled-force prior the same
comparison gives $-3.5\%$ with a per-seed spread of $18.3\%$ ($t=0.4$, $2/4$): no
effect. So we report the frozen-trunk advantage for the parameter-error case only, and
read it as the trunk overfitting the majority regime when it is allowed to keep
training on data whose mismatch lives in the minority windows.

The same heads also let us test whether the interference floor is a property of the
architecture or of the training mixture. On the pendulum the residual targets of four
priors span $100\times$, and the head's free-flight output tracks its target in every
one of them, and the two ends of the ladder are the informative part. Averaged over seeds the ratio is $0.98 \pm 0.02$ at the coarsest tier and $0.13 \pm 0.01$ at the exact tier ($n{=}7$ each), a separation of $z{=}48$; at the exact tier the target is $0.0004$ normalised and the head emits $0.00005$. The intermediate tiers lie between them ($0.96$ over $7$ seeds, and $0.77 \pm 0.11$ over the $3$ seeds we ran for the coarse-RK4 tier). In the ball world the same ratio runs from
$1.0$ to $1.4\times10^{5}$. The floor is therefore not a general property of a shared residual
head: it appears when the minority window's target is small relative to the majority's,
which is the ball's situation (contact targets near $0.015$ against free-flight targets
near $1.4\times10^{-7}$) and not the pendulum's (all targets the same order). This is
why \S\ref{sec:silence}'s measurement is reported for the contact-rich world alone.

We also carried \S\ref{sec:silence}'s decode rule into this setting, since the pendulum
exposes an analogous contact channel through its phase head and is $88\%$ free flight.
The rule is monotonically harmful here. Raising the threshold so that it actually closes
windows moves closed-loop error back toward the prior: from $0.541$ ungated to $0.586$
with every free-flight window silenced under the wrong-parameter prior, and from
$0.157$ to $0.262$ under the unmodelled force. The reason is the same fact that makes the
head useful in this appendix---when the head beats the prior in \emph{both} regimes, a
rule that silences it on one of them can only remove work. The gate is therefore not a
general improvement but a response to a specific condition, namely the head being net
harmful on the regime it silences; that condition is what \S\ref{sec:silence} measures in
the ball world, and stating it as a condition rather than a technique is what we take to
be the transferable part.

